  "summary": "Traditional townhouses of Kyoto designed for narrow lots with deep interiors.",
  "description": "Characterized by long, narrow layouts with street-facing facades, wooden lattice fronts (mado), and interior courtyards (tsuboniwa). Developed during Edo period as urban housing for merchants and artisans, maximizing limited space while maintaining connection to nature.",
  "culturalContext": "Kyoto's machiya — merchant townhouses built narrow-fronted (\"eel's beds\", unagi no nedoko) because street frontage was taxed — packed workshops, earthen-floored passage (tori-niwa), latticed shopfronts and domestic courtyards into 5-12 meter widths. Wooden lattices (koshi), inuyarai fire barriers, hidden gardens and tsuji-bei mud walls encode merchant prudence and craft pride; hundreds survive in Nishijin and Gion, now protected, restored and adapted into cafés and craft shops.",
